#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10418" :question/problem "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can choose to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $25\\%$ of the customers, and $60\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $30\\%$ of the customers, and $30\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $45\\%$ of the customers, and $20\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\nIf $65\\%$ of all customers travel with children, and $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer travels with children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and $I_1, I_2, I_3$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.3 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.45 &  & P(B|{I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $I_1 \\sqcup I_2  \\sqcup I_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(I_1)+ P(I_2) + P(I_3) = 0.25+0.3+0.45 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C) = 0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B | C) = 0.4.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children, which is represented as $P(C | B).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.65)}{(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.3)+(0.2)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.26}{0.33} \\\\  & ≈0.788,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" :question/math-academy-id "e-8911" :question/problem "The disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ of events $A_1,A_2,A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_1|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.23, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.26, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.18, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,\\ldots,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP(A_1) + P(A_2) + P(A_3) = 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.4) \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.23)(0.3)}{(0.23)(0.3)+(0.26)(0.3)+(0.18)(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.069}{0.219} \\\\  & ≈0.315.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8912" :question/problem "Potatoes produced by a particular farm come in three different varieties, variety $1,$ variety $2,$ and variety $3.$\n\n- $36\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $1,$ and $20\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $17\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $2,$ and $6\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $47\\%$ of the potatoes are of the variety $3.$\n\nIf $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, $40\\%$ of potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, what is the probability that a randomly selected potato from variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a potato is sold to the restaurant, $W$ be the event that a potato weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $V_1, V_2, V_3$ be the event that a potato is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.36 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.17 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $V_1 \\sqcup V_2  \\sqcup V_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(V_1)+ P(V_2) + P(V_3) = 0.36+0.17+0.47 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = 0.16.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W | R) = 0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | W) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen potato of variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ which is represented as $P(W | V_3).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.16)}{(0.2)(0.36)+(0.06)(0.17)+P(W|{V}_{3})(0.47)} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{0.064}{0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W | V_3),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15(0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})) & =0.064 \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.064}{0.15} \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75} \\\\ 0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822 \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.47}(\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822) \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & ≈0.733\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8dac953-8c37-59e7-8fe0-02995240c8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163769} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a3e076-7caa-5547-9a00-710bdb26a958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163770} {:answer/id #uuid "0065bdc3-5a85-580a-b646-c0c3f8b3a082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.427" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163771} {:answer/id #uuid "378ab112-fb34-5f2f-a941-6b455f5fc293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.435" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163772} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa843b3-4375-5967-9938-b21632b67e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa843b3-4375-5967-9938-b21632b67e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72dc4b4-bf09-5d67-8671-75165506e5e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7f933e2-5017-50df-b8ef-7fd429d6b6ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-147126" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.4, & P({A}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.5, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.3, & P({A}_{3})=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.2+0.3+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.4)(0.2)+(0.5)(0.3)+(0.3)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.38} \\\\  & ≈0.395,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adaca2a-25b8-5492-8e73-6d48d5c107c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486615} {:answer/id #uuid "5becaaa9-5f26-54d9-87bd-69282364be70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486800} {:answer/id #uuid "75086fb7-7e32-5fdd-b1ef-015370496b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486848} {:answer/id #uuid "7872a91f-ffad-5a9d-bb60-967650a7e581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486858} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ab6e75-9266-51d3-85df-bd426a296750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75086fb7-7e32-5fdd-b1ef-015370496b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486848} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b922273-f540-5f67-8f2c-45b151c3e563" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43f08f68-330a-59ee-956f-33e7cbacb451" :question/math-academy-id "q-147882" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{8}, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=1, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})+(1)(\\frac{7}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{20})}{(\\frac{9}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed428b8-6f18-5ff1-90d2-8ef1dde6f087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486763} {:answer/id #uuid "63796697-34fe-55d5-8f2c-7ab6e52b5aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486814} {:answer/id #uuid "650ee92d-5ff6-58b3-8008-961a97a64380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486817} {:answer/id #uuid "e25fa73b-0199-5b58-be51-b0ae763bb736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487088} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f4a100-d5b2-5011-a8a9-5c2ed4569149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ed428b8-6f18-5ff1-90d2-8ef1dde6f087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1087d30-390e-5dbd-ad7c-e5cfba347e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "855a7cb7-0048-5462-8815-114b332521cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-147886" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0, & P({A}_{1})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.6, & P({A}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.3, & P({A}_{3})=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.25+0.25+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)(0.25)}{(0)(0.25)+(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a9f222-5e6c-584f-8a20-252c749fb9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486752} {:answer/id #uuid "5a778b05-75eb-5814-bf54-a782e3521987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486795} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6aee04-ab75-5dd5-ac10-45761ebcebf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2037c8-40f3-5d56-9c3b-51a6e2eb5517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487043} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd5001a-0138-50b7-bf54-a87a859ada26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b6aee04-ab75-5dd5-ac10-45761ebcebf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77d5683-3c91-57b4-a6c6-baacce64e39e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c768c6df-9213-55c1-bcb4-06af864aa6e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147887" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=\\frac{2}{5}, & P({A}_{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})+(0)(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{40})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23203699-becd-55da-b691-94c483c1502e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486663} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc57ec1-be1c-5d1f-ad98-1cdf409dbf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486724} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf6502e-5be3-5355-863c-ac97ffde7666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486865} {:answer/id #uuid "87290b0a-61ff-5a41-b47f-b61c5a48f980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.037" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486887} {:answer/id #uuid "df982389-6d70-5939-9ddd-222055d362bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23203699-becd-55da-b691-94c483c1502e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486663} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f73bac-72ca-5eba-9074-1537533d7a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25486da2-4aed-57cd-8065-4f05b0f0cbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-147889" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.75, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.15, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.8, & P({A}_{3})=0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.6) \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{(0.75)(0.1)+(0.15)(0.3)+(0.8)(0.6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} {:answer/id #uuid "a059a8b3-f1ed-5de8-a334-40e4ba40f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.602" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486959} {:answer/id #uuid "a6776cbd-ebfc-53b8-ad96-0550911cd2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486972} {:answer/id #uuid "ec76cd0f-4509-5d07-9c06-06e62501a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.672" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487097} {:answer/id #uuid "efaec769-99d0-583b-aad4-773115f51b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e23228-7c0d-5766-b1ab-6e8ce201deb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148079" :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ee0324-47ce-520c-be1b-617fc28bde99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486628} {:answer/id #uuid "1f964113-d9c4-53ce-a730-891983a25308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486654} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} {:answer/id #uuid "56ac6ed1-d21c-50b6-90e0-44a21cfc1766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486783} {:answer/id #uuid "8146efe8-c4c6-5ac7-a7c5-ddfd418eda3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bc298-c38f-59f1-b1e6-e063f2062f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-148085" :question/problem "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b28de4-aa80-5f7f-a8f5-b4f77a28ca63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486638} {:answer/id #uuid "6dca3446-f03b-53af-ba74-95ebd9f19f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486836} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d76d72-edbb-5e01-b342-efdf9523a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487127} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ffd134-c98f-5abb-a2f0-f7d9bf8bae1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487128} {:answer/id #uuid "faeb72a2-110a-55d3-ba57-0a887a322d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13b28de4-aa80-5f7f-a8f5-b4f77a28ca63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486638} :answer-field/id #uuid "00cf1d61-f08e-59ba-9b21-977d6711da5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "521e5a18-fa22-58cf-9f82-7b4aa14a1323" :question/math-academy-id "q-148152" :question/problem "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea9c991-6d0c-58cc-a1c9-673f7df321be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486651} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee60891-3781-507e-bc5f-6ec3ac29955e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486653} {:answer/id #uuid "9787bb0c-ac29-5445-8788-e2cf78f4adfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486935} {:answer/id #uuid "aee1e2c8-d3aa-559e-960b-7c37d12644d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.511" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486985} {:answer/id #uuid "e54bfffb-085e-55df-a079-84de70fd4e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.596" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea9c991-6d0c-58cc-a1c9-673f7df321be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486651} :answer-field/id #uuid "c98b88dd-4fa4-5b39-9489-836aa4342061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42158c74-7958-54df-8e89-80fc77114095" :question/math-academy-id "q-148270" :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $55%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $52%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42%$ of students are female, $80%$ of female students write in print, and $56%$ of students that write in print are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75d92088-a5b2-5e87-ac81-9a5b9f8f3276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486849} {:answer/id #uuid "80ee89b4-f1a5-5c18-8a6e-5aae77ccefea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.338" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486874} {:answer/id #uuid "96ea620f-8131-5da2-9bb4-240fcf0ef5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.383" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486930} {:answer/id #uuid "c311c35e-29b6-52eb-9113-8fbebbcf4b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.377" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487026} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd4a4cd-de69-5d9e-935c-7b6c502bb7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ea620f-8131-5da2-9bb4-240fcf0ef5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.383" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486930} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1b300f-8550-5161-bdfe-afedec77711a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4190f4-bdf3-5375-9c0a-f6bde72a3f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-148273" :question/problem "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers that play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play the piano, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $15%$ of teenagers play the violin.\n\n- $65%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $35%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $12%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, and $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that plays the violin is good at math? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.65 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.2+0.15+0.65=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.12.\n$$\n\n- Since $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.27.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|M)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who plays the violin is good at math, which is represented as $P(M|{T}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\ P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{(0.27)(0.12)}{(0.2)(0.6)+P(M|{T}_{2})(0.15)+(0.35)(0.65)} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(M|{T}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.08(0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})) & =0.0324 \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.08} \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200} \\\\ 0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475 \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.15}(\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475) \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & ≈0.383\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08940ee3-c31c-52b4-8370-33f3d3aa8657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.475" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486610} {:answer/id #uuid "1faf867c-bba7-5f95-927b-c813a9e9dcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.623" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486656} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa3b441-f3be-5a7b-8222-c944862c87a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486692} {:answer/id #uuid "6eabcb60-c57c-5edd-bee7-61fe19a4610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486838} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1bcac5-e08b-5cb7-8e74-64d1c2bee8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff1bcac5-e08b-5cb7-8e74-64d1c2bee8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.740" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487137} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4501a8-443f-5ae0-aa78-dccc204637f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b22d29b-922e-508b-b541-5acf3087f769" :question/math-academy-id "q-148523" :question/problem "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cab1720-3224-5447-8d79-5bf013546a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.178" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486721} {:answer/id #uuid "a42506fe-6559-5f2b-8c06-aa18fee58039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486966} {:answer/id #uuid "af17e71a-93bd-5a7f-af3c-305eac11006d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.155" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486986} {:answer/id #uuid "b2acddda-e825-5194-bcc0-7a5da49b9c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486990} {:answer/id #uuid "c3899e6b-aff1-5bcb-b375-2828bb7bf8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cab1720-3224-5447-8d79-5bf013546a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.178" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486721} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f2927d-e92c-503c-bbdb-bb37d44e2ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1462b070-fed9-586f-924d-db4fb0fa8a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148664" :question/problem "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two light bulb types: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $2%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $50%$ of the entire production, and $3%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, and $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1fa001-de5a-5eca-a293-68e34ff2a57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486677} {:answer/id #uuid "2e737bb5-286a-5736-abef-99238ad177e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.063" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486684} {:answer/id #uuid "36574732-0df7-5034-8f03-50f5c578f100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.037" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486710} {:answer/id #uuid "3e11e93a-1df8-5c32-bedc-80b52a2f4333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486726} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9e95c1-39bf-5501-a538-4ede4bedceff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e11e93a-1df8-5c32-bedc-80b52a2f4333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486726} :answer-field/id #uuid "a35ad5a8-56d1-57e8-9fd7-f2fcd100b7cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47b23978-faf0-57d0-85ed-6efe7d74f281" :question/math-academy-id "q-148667" :question/problem "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ buys $33%$ of the copies, and $90%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $2$ buys $25%$ of the copies, and $87%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ buys $42%$ of the copies, and $82%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $5%$ of all copies have some printing defect, and $70%$ of defective copies are sold, what is the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is bought by distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.33 &  & P(S|{D}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.87 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.42 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.33+0.25+0.42=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.05.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect, which is represented as $P(R|S).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.05)}{(0.9)(0.33)+(0.87)(0.25)+(0.82)(0.42)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.035}{0.8589} \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05268a54-1ca3-5236-8a32-72d18477c132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.463" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486603} {:answer/id #uuid "2663f13b-e38f-5407-950a-5fe65e600172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486669} {:answer/id #uuid "3a52d0ba-3076-551b-ab78-67b5cf870df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486719} {:answer/id #uuid "a5af02ee-9d40-5c6e-b134-2b707a5335a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486971} {:answer/id #uuid "afb3e40b-8ac2-504a-9793-b673024033ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.904" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2663f13b-e38f-5407-950a-5fe65e600172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486669} :answer-field/id #uuid "698fc6a4-867b-5609-8d53-bcc56debe7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa54d1c3-840f-55dc-9c9a-7cb1c9435baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159358" :question/problem "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and itinerary $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can decide to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $60%$ of the customers, and $10%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers, and $25%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers.\n\nIf $30%$ of all customers travel with children, $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, and $83%$ of customers that choose the bus travel with children, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ takes the bus? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a05142-e44c-540a-9c03-163903193327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486647} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8ab37c-6a45-5646-a710-ef97c29c42d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486691} {:answer/id #uuid "486fb372-cfeb-5d0b-826b-d22904bdda5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486751} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f563cf-cbac-55d9-ad4a-9b25558f407b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487038} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d7becd-94e1-50d0-844f-30f47d1cb01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19a05142-e44c-540a-9c03-163903193327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486647} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d856b94-8d50-5639-8d1c-3c6881156a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c74ed7c-bf09-54d6-a2a8-5c3ba0194b49" :question/math-academy-id "q-170685" :question/problem "A farm sells its apples to three grocery stores; $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Store $1$ buys $14%$ of the apples.\n\n- Store $2$ buys $26%$ of the apples, and $50%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\n- Store $3$ buys $60%$ of the apples, and $25%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\nIf $63%$ of all apples are red, $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, and $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that an apple is red, $S$ be the event that the apple is sold in one day, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3}$ be the event that an apple is sold by store $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{1})=0.14 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.26 &  & P(S|{S}_{2})=0.5 \\\\  & P({S}_{3})=0.60 &  & P(S|{S}_{3})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})=0.14+0.26+0.60=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $63%$ of all apples are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.63.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.45.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day, which is represented as $P(S|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(S|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(S|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{(0.63)(0.25)}{P(S|{S}_{1})(0.14)+(0.5)(0.26)+(0.25)(0.60)} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.45(0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28) & =0.1575 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.45} \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =0.35 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.35-0.28 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.07 \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.07}{0.14} \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
